{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the first month.</b> After He numbered the men of the army and arranged the banners and the bearers of the Mishkan in order to bring them into the land, and purified their camp from the impure, as it says: וְהָיָה מַחֲנֶיךָ קָדוֹשׁ (Deuteronomy 23:15), and from the mamzerim through the matter of the sota, so that the Shekhinah would be among them in the camp of their hosts — Scripture recounts four good deeds that Israel did, through which they merited to enter the land immediately without war, were it not for the spies, as Moses our teacher testified in saying to Hobab: נֹסְעִים אֲנַחְנוּ אֶל הַמָּקוֹם (Numbers 10:29). First, he recounted the dedication of the altar. Second, their zeal in the matter of the inauguration of the Levites. Third, their zeal in the paschal offering. Fourth, their going after the blessed El in the wilderness, even though the cloud's rising was at unknown times — sometimes long, sometimes short — such that encampment and journeying should have been very difficult for them. And to make all these known, Scripture recounted them according to the degree of their being favorable before Him, not according to the time in which they occurred. Therefore he recounted here the matter of the dedication of the altar and the Levites and the Pesach, which were in the first month, though the beginning of the book was in the second month, and the matter of their encampment and journeying at HaShem's word was from the day they came out of Egypt. On this principle the Sages said: אֵין מֻקְדָּם וּמְאֻחָר בַּתּוֹרָה (there is no earlier and later in the Torah) — and this when the arrangement of what is narrated intends some end other than chronological order."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:39:09.620310180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}